    <item>
      <title>Solved: How to Merge or Join Data Frames in R</title>
      <link>https://sqlpey.com/r/solved-how-to-merge-or-join-data-frames-in-r/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 23 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://sqlpey.com/r/solved-how-to-merge-or-join-data-frames-in-r/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 id=&#34;solved-how-to-merge-or-join-data-frames-in-r&#34;&gt;&amp;ldquo;Solved: How to Merge or Join Data Frames in R&amp;rdquo;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;h3 id=&#34;setting-up-sample-datasets&#34;&gt;Setting Up Sample Datasets&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;To illustrate the different joining techniques, let&amp;rsquo;s create a couple of sample R data frames. These examples will be used throughout the solutions presented below.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-R&#34; data-lang=&#34;R&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# Data Frame 1: Sales inventory&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;sales_inventory &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;data.frame&lt;/span&gt;(&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  CustomerID_Key &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;),&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  ItemType &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;rep&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;PowerMixer&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;), &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;rep&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;AudioDevice&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;))&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# Data Frame 2: Customer geographical data&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# Note: Using L for integer literals for consistency where appropriate&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;customer_geo_data &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;data.frame&lt;/span&gt;(&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  CustomerID_Key &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2L&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;4L&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;7L&lt;/span&gt;),&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;  StateLocation &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;rep&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;California&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;), &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;rep&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Texas&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;))&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;# Display initial data frames for review&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Sample Data Frame 1 (sales_inventory):&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;(sales_inventory)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;(&lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;\nSample Data Frame 2 (customer_geo_data):&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;(customer_geo_data)&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;h3 id=&#34;method-1-base-rs-merge-function-for-flexible-joins&#34;&gt;Method 1: Base R&amp;rsquo;s &lt;code&gt;merge()&lt;/code&gt; Function for Flexible Joins&lt;/h3&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;merge()&lt;/code&gt; function in base R is a versatile tool for combining data frames. It automatically identifies common column names for joining if not explicitly specified. However, for precise control and clarity, it&amp;rsquo;s generally best practice to define the join columns.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
